{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Constitution of Okaiken''' ({{nihongo|岡井県国憲法|Okaiken-Koku Kenpō}}) is the fundamental law of [[Okaiken]]. It was enacted on May 4, 1970, and has been amended thirteen times. For the history of the Constitution and its amendments, see [[Constitutional history of Okaiken]].

==Preamble==

We, the Sanese people, acting through our duly elected representatives in the Constitutional Diet of 1969, determined that we shall establish a nation founded upon the truth of what has happened and the resolve that it shall not happen again—

Acknowledging that the government of Genzui Kusaka, having obtained power through fraud, sought to abolish the elected parliament and establish a one-party state, and that this attempt was defeated only by the courage of the Sanese people, the defection of the Okami Capital Defense Force, and the intervention of Emperor Wakayama, who called for peace and affirmed that the will of the people must be respected;

Acknowledging that Emperor Wakayama, having prevented civil war, voluntarily offered to abdicate his political authority, making possible the transition from empire to republic without bloodshed;

Acknowledging that the Saito Constitution failed because it placed the military beyond civilian control, and that the Great Adonian War demonstrated the catastrophic consequences of that failure;

Determined that no future government shall capture the electoral system to perpetuate its rule, that no military shall operate independently of civilian authority, and that the people shall possess the means to amend their own Constitution when the ordinary processes of government fail;

Resolved that the nation shall be built upon resilience—the capacity of the people and the state to withstand adversity, to recover from injury, and to develop the conditions under which all persons may contribute to the common welfare;

Proclaiming that the Sanese people—named for the Three Lands of the archipelago, Harujima, Ishijima, and Yagikoshu—constitute a sovereign nation, administered as the domain of Okaiken (岡井県), the hill upon which the capital of Okami stands;

Do establish this Constitution of Okaiken.

==Chapter I: The Republic and the Emperor==

'''Article 1.''' Sovereign power resides with the people of Okaiken. The Republic shall be governed by the elected representatives of the people, through the Diet, the Cabinet, and the Judiciary.
''(Amended 1971, Second Amendment: The Emperor shall be the symbol of the Sanese identity, deriving his position from the will of the people with whom resides sovereign power. The Emperor shall have no political authority.)''
''(Amended 2011, Jutsu Restoration — Q1: The Emperor shall be the Head of State and the symbol of the unity of the People, deriving his position from the will of the people with whom resides sovereign power.)''

'''Article 1A.''' ''(Added 2011, Jutsu Restoration — Q3)'' The Emperor shall exercise reserve powers only when the Diet has failed to perform its constitutional functions, as defined herein:
:(a) When the Diet has failed to pass a budget within 120 days of the start of the fiscal year, the Emperor may, upon the advice of the Cabinet, dissolve the House of Commoners and order new elections.
:(b) When the Diet has failed to designate a Prime Minister within 30 days of a vacancy, the Emperor may designate a Prime Minister from among the members of the Diet.
:(c) When a budgetary deadlock between the House of Officers and the House of Commoners cannot be resolved through the normal legislative process, the Emperor may, upon the advice of the Cabinet, certify the budget as passed by the House of Commoners.

'''Article 1B.''' ''(Added 2011, Jutsu Restoration — Q3)'' The Emperor shall not exercise reserve powers for any purpose other than the restoration of the constitutional order. The Emperor's reserve powers may not be used to influence the content of legislation, to appoint or remove Ministers of State, or to override the will of the Diet as expressed through a duly passed law. Any exercise of reserve powers shall be reported to the Diet within 48 hours and shall be subject to review by the Supreme Court.

'''Article 2.''' The Imperial Throne shall be dynastic and succeeded to in accordance with the Law of the Imperial Succession passed by the Diet.

'''Article 3.''' The advice and approval of the Cabinet shall be required for all acts of the Emperor in matters of state, and the Cabinet shall be responsible therefor.

'''Article 4.''' The Emperor shall perform only such acts in matters of state as are provided for in this Constitution and shall not have powers related to government. The Emperor may delegate the performance of his acts in matters of state as may be provided by law.

'''Article 5.''' When, in accordance with the Law of the Imperial Succession, a Regency is established, the Regent shall perform acts in matters of state in the Emperor's name. In this case, the preceding article shall not apply.

'''Article 6.''' The Emperor shall appoint the Prime Minister as designated by the Diet. The Emperor shall appoint the Chief Justice of the Supreme Court as designated by the Cabinet.

'''Article 7.''' The Emperor, with the advice and approval of the Cabinet, shall perform the following acts in matters of state on behalf of the people:
:Promulgation of amendments of the Constitution, laws, executive directives and treaties.
:Convocation of the Diet.
:Dissolution of the House of Commoners.
:Proclamation of general election of members of the Diet.
:Certification of the appointment and dismissal of Ministers of State and other officials as provided for by law, and of full powers and credentials of Ambassadors and Ministers.
:Certification of general and special amnesty, commutation of punishment, reprieve, and restoration of rights.
:Awarding of honors.
:Certification of instruments of ratification and other diplomatic documents as provided for by law.
:Receiving foreign ambassadors and ministers.
:Proclamation of emergency referendums as provided for in Chapter IX.
:Performance of ceremonial functions.

'''Article 8.''' All property of the Imperial Household shall belong to the State. No property can be given to, or received by, the Imperial House, nor can any gifts be made therefrom, except in accordance with the resolutions of the Diet.

==Chapter II: Renunciation of War==

''(Original 1970 text: Chapter II — The Military. Articles 9–12 established the Prime Minister as Commander-in-Chief, the Self-Defense Forces as a permanent institution under civilian control, the prohibition on military independence, and the prohibition on domestic deployment for suppression of dissent. Replaced in its entirety by the Ninth Amendment, 1984.)''

'''Article 9.''' ''(Ninth Amendment, 1984)'' Aspiring sincerely to an international peace based on justice and order, the Sanese people forever renounce war as a sovereign right of the nation and the threat or use of force as a means of settling international disputes.
(2) In order to accomplish the aim of the preceding paragraph, land, sea, and air forces, as well as other war potential, will never be maintained. The right of belligerency of the state will not be recognized.
(3) The Sanese people shall not permit the deployment of Sanese military personnel overseas for combat operations, even under Adonian Community authorization. The Self-Defense Forces shall be maintained solely for the defense of the Sanese archipelago and the protection of its territorial waters.

''(Paragraph (2) repealed by the Thirteenth Amendment, 2014. Paragraph (3) amended by the Thirteenth Amendment, 2014: "In order to accomplish the aim of the preceding paragraph, land, sea and air forces are to be maintained to protect Okaiken's most valuable assets. The Sanese people retain the right to protect their assets and their foreign allies from threats of intimidation and war.")''

==Chapter III: Rights and Duties of the People==

'''Article 10.''' The conditions necessary for being a Sanese national shall be determined by law.

'''Article 11.''' The people shall not be prevented from enjoying any of the fundamental human rights. These fundamental human rights are conferred upon this and future generations as eternal and inviolate rights, and shall be maintained by the constant endeavor of the people.

'''Article 12.''' The freedoms and rights guaranteed to the people by this Constitution shall be maintained by the constant endeavor of the people, who shall refrain from any abuse of these freedoms and rights and shall always be responsible for utilizing them for the common welfare.
(2) All persons shall maintain their capacity to contribute to the common welfare, and shall refrain from the abuse of rights and freedoms.

'''Article 12A.''' The exercise of rights and freedoms shall be consistent with the maintenance of social harmony and the common welfare. No person shall exercise their rights in a manner that substantially impairs the capacity of the community to function as a whole.
(2) The principle of social harmony shall not be invoked to suppress lawful dissent, to penalize the expression of minority viewpoints, or to enforce conformity of thought, appearance, or association. The courts shall determine, on a case-by-case basis, whether a restriction on rights is justified by the principle of social harmony, applying the standard of necessity and proportionality.

'''Article 13.''' All of the people shall be respected as individuals. Their right to life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness shall, to the extent that it does not interfere with the common welfare, be the supreme consideration in legislation and in other governmental affairs.

'''Article 14.''' All persons are equal under the law. No person shall suffer adverse treatment by the State on the basis of their relational status, their gender expression, or their participation in lawful association. The law shall protect the free exercise of relationships and expressions, and shall not classify citizens by identity.

'''Article 15.''' The people have the inalienable right to choose their public officials and to dismiss them. All public officials are servants of the whole community and not of any group thereof. Universal adult suffrage is guaranteed with regard to the election of public officials. In all elections, secrecy of the ballot shall not be violated. A voter shall not be held answerable, publicly or privately, for the choice made. The Electoral Commission shall administer all national elections in accordance with Chapter VIII.

'''Article 15A.''' Political parties shall register with the Electoral Commission, disclose their membership and financing, and are prohibited from advocating the violent overthrow of the constitutional order. No political party may be banned except by judicial order, following a finding that the party has violated these conditions.

'''Article 16.''' Every person shall have the right of peaceful petition for the redress of damage, the removal of a public official, the enactment, repeal or amendment of a law, ordinance or regulation, or for any other purpose. No person shall be in any way held responsible for any such petition which has not been made simply by resort to intimidation or violence.

'''Article 16A.''' The right of petition extends to the initiation of emergency referendums under Chapter IX of this Constitution. When a petition signed by at least ten percent (10%) of registered voters is submitted to the Electoral Commission, the Commission shall verify the signatures within thirty (30) days and, if the threshold is met, shall certify the petition as valid. Upon certification, the emergency referendum process of Chapter IX shall be initiated.
(2) The Electoral Commission shall maintain a public register of all petitions submitted under this article, including the number of signatures, the date of submission, and the disposition of each petition.

'''Article 17.''' Every person may sue for redress in accordance with the law from the State or a public entity, in case he has suffered damage through an illegal act of any public official.

'''Article 18.''' No person shall be held in bondage of any kind. Involuntary servitude, except as punishment for crime, is prohibited.

'''Article 19.''' Freedom of thought and conscience shall not be violated.

'''Article 20.''' Freedom of religion is guaranteed to all. No religious organization shall receive any privileges from the State nor exercise any political authority. No person shall be compelled to take part in any religious act, celebration, rite or practice. The State and its organs shall refrain from religious education or any other religious activity.

'''Article 21.''' Freedom of assembly and association as well as speech, press and all other forms of expression are guaranteed. No censorship shall be maintained, nor shall the secrecy of any means of communication be violated.

'''Article 22.''' Every person shall have freedom to choose and change his residence and to choose his occupation to the extent that it does not interfere with the common welfare. Freedom of all persons to move to a foreign country and to divest themselves of their nationality shall be inviolate.

'''Article 23.''' Freedom of research, inquiry, and intellectual endeavor is guaranteed.

'''Article 24.''' Marriage and companion registration shall be based only on the mutual consent of both parties and shall be maintained through mutual cooperation with the equal rights of the partners as a basis.
(2) With regard to choice of spouse or companion, property rights, inheritance, choice of domicile, dissolution and other matters pertaining to marriage, companion registration, and the family, laws shall be enacted from the standpoint of individual dignity and the essential equality of the parties.

'''Article 25.''' All people shall have the right to maintain the minimum standards of resilient living—sufficient to withstand adversity, to recover from injury, and to develop the capacity for self-reliance.
(2) In all spheres of life, the State shall use its endeavors for the promotion and extension of social welfare and security, and of public health.
(3) The State shall maintain the conditions under which all persons may develop their physical, mental, and professional capacity. The right of access to healthcare, to education, to digital infrastructure, and to physical training facilities shall be secured by law.

'''Article 26.''' All people shall have the right to receive an equal education correspondent to their ability, as provided by law. All people shall be obligated to have all boys and girls under their protection receive ordinary education as provided for by law. Such compulsory education shall be free.

'''Article 26A.''' All persons shall have the right to a healthy and sustainable environment. The State and the people shall maintain the physical conditions of the archipelago—its waters, forests, soils, air, and mineral resources—as a trust for future generations.
(2) The State shall prevent the degradation of the natural environment and shall remediate environmental harm caused by prior industrial activity.
(3) No person shall discharge pollutants into the common environment beyond limits established by law. The obligation of environmental stewardship extends to all persons, corporations, and instruments of the State.

'''Article 26B.''' The maintenance of public order is a shared obligation of the State and the people. Citizens shall cooperate with law enforcement in the prevention and detection of crime, and shall participate in community safety organizations as provided by law.
(2) The State shall maintain civilian police forces under the authority of the Cabinet. No military organization shall exercise police powers within the territory of Okaiken, except as provided in Article 12.
(3) Neighborhood associations ({{nihongo|町内会|chōnaikai}}) and other community organizations may assist in disaster preparedness, crime prevention, and public safety, provided that such assistance does not constitute the exercise of police powers by private actors. The organization, authority, and limits of community safety organizations shall be determined by law.

'''Article 26C.''' All citizens shall have the obligation to serve the nation. National service shall include military service, civil defense, disaster response, and community service as determined by law.
(2) The State shall maintain a system of national service in which all citizens between the ages of eighteen and thirty-five shall serve for a period determined by law, not to exceed twenty-four months. The conditions and categories of service shall be determined by law, provided that no citizen shall be compelled to bear arms against their conscience.
(3) Citizens who have completed national service shall be entitled to the benefits of the Resilience Guarantee: priority access to public employment, educational subsidies, and housing assistance, as provided by law.

'''Article 27.''' All people shall have the right and the obligation to work. The right to work is the right to contribute; the obligation to work is the obligation to sustain the community that sustains you.
(2) Standards for wages, hours, rest and other working conditions shall be fixed by law.
(3) Children shall not be exploited.

'''Article 28.''' The right of workers to organize and to bargain and act collectively is guaranteed.

'''Article 29.''' The right to own or to hold property is inviolable.
(2) Property rights shall be defined by law, in conformity with the common welfare.
(3) Private property may be taken for public use upon just compensation therefor.
(4) The State may coordinate economic activity through administrative guidance, provided that such guidance is subject to judicial review and does not constitute arbitrary deprivation of property or liberty. The State shall maintain digital sovereignty as a strategic interest of the nation.
(5) Corporations chartered under the laws of Okaiken shall bear obligations to their workers and to the nation proportional to the privileges of incorporation.

'''Article 30.''' The people shall be liable to taxation as provided by law.

'''Article 31.''' No person shall be deprived of life or liberty, nor shall any other criminal penalty be imposed, except according to procedure established by law. The purpose of criminal law is to protect the community and to restore the capacity of the offender; punishment that destroys without the possibility of recovery is contrary to the principles of this Constitution.

'''Article 32.''' No person shall be denied the right of access to the courts.

'''Article 33.''' No person shall be apprehended except upon warrant issued by a competent judicial officer which specifies the offense with which the person is charged, unless he is apprehended in the act of committing the offense.

'''Article 34.''' No person shall be arrested or detained without being at once informed of the charges against him or without the immediate privilege of counsel; nor shall he be detained without adequate cause; and upon demand of any person such cause must be immediately shown in open court in his presence and the presence of his counsel.

'''Article 35.''' The right of all persons to be secure in their homes, papers and effects against entries, searches and seizures shall not be impaired except upon warrant issued for adequate cause and particularly describing the place to be searched and things to be seized, or except as provided by Article 33. Each search or seizure shall be made upon separate warrant issued by a competent judicial officer.

'''Article 36.''' The infliction of torture by any public officer and acts of cruelty are absolutely forbidden.

'''Article 37.''' In all criminal cases the accused shall enjoy the right to a speedy and public trial by an impartial tribunal, the right of counsel, and the right to examine witnesses. At all times the accused shall have the assistance of competent counsel who shall, if the accused is unable to secure the same by his own efforts, be assigned to his use by the State.

'''Article 38.''' No person shall be compelled to testify against himself. Confession made under compulsion, torture or threat, or after prolonged arrest or detention shall not be admitted in evidence. No person shall be convicted or punished in cases where the only proof against him is his own confession.

'''Article 39.''' No person shall be held criminally liable for an act which was lawful at the time it was committed, or of which he has been acquitted, nor shall he be placed in double jeopardy.

'''Article 40.''' Any person, in case he is acquitted after he has been arrested or detained, may sue the State for redress as provided by law.

'''Article 40A.''' The State shall maintain a system of criminal justice that seeks the rehabilitation and reintegration of offenders. Imprisonment shall not be imposed where lesser measures are sufficient to protect the community. The conditions of imprisonment shall preserve the dignity and capacity of the person, and shall not destroy the possibility of recovery. Upon release, the State shall facilitate the reintegration of the person into the community, including access to employment, housing, and social services.

==Chapter IV: The Diet==

'''Article 41.''' The Diet shall be the highest organ of state power, and shall be the sole law-making organ of the State.

'''Article 42.''' The Diet shall consist of two Houses, namely the House of Officers and the House of Commoners.

'''Article 43.''' Both Houses shall consist of elected members, representatives of all the people. The House of Officers shall be elected through professional and industry associations as provided by law. The House of Commoners shall be elected by direct vote of the people.

'''Article 44.''' The qualifications of members of both Houses and their electors shall be fixed by law. However, there shall be no discrimination because of race, creed, sex, social status, family origin, education, property or income.

'''Article 45.''' The term of office of members of the House of Commoners shall be four years.

'''Article 46.''' The term of office of members of the House of Officers shall be six years, and election for half the members shall take place every three years.

'''Article 47.''' Electoral districts, method of voting and other matters pertaining to the method of election of members of both Houses shall be fixed by law. The Electoral Commission shall administer all national elections in accordance with Chapter VIII.

'''Article 48.''' No person shall simultaneously be a member of both Houses.

'''Article 49.''' Members of both Houses shall receive appropriate annual payment from the national treasury in accordance with law.

'''Article 50.''' Except in cases provided by law, members of both Houses shall be exempt from apprehension while the Diet is in session, and any members apprehended before the opening of the session shall be freed during the term of the session upon demand of the House.

'''Article 51.''' Members of both Houses shall not be held liable outside the House for speeches, debates or votes cast inside the House.

'''Article 52.''' An ordinary session of the Diet shall be convoked once per year.

'''Article 53.''' The Cabinet may determine to convoke extraordinary sessions of the Diet. When a quarter or more of the total members of either House makes the demand, the Cabinet must determine on such convocation.

'''Article 54.''' When the House of Commoners is dissolved, there must be a general election of members of the House of Commoners within forty (40) days from the date of dissolution, and the Diet must be convoked within thirty (30) days from the date of the election.

'''Article 55.''' Each House shall judge disputes related to qualifications of its members. However, in order to challenge the election of a member, a complaint must be presented to the Electoral Commission, which shall investigate and report its findings to the House within sixty (60) days.

'''Article 56.''' Business cannot be transacted in either House unless one-third or more of total membership is present.

'''Article 57.''' All proceedings of both Houses shall be public, and a verbatim record of proceedings shall be published and made available to the public. Upon the demand of one-fifth or more of the members present, the vote of each member on any matter shall be recorded in the minutes.

'''Article 58.''' Each House shall select its own president and other officials. Each House shall establish its rules pertaining to meetings, proceedings and internal discipline, and may punish members for disorderly conduct. However, in order to expel a member, a majority of two-thirds or more of those members present must agree.

'''Article 59.''' A bill becomes a law on passage by both Houses, except as otherwise provided by the Constitution.
(2) A bill which is passed by the House of Commoners, and upon which the House of Officers makes a decision different from that of the House of Commoners, becomes a law when passed a second time by the House of Commoners by a majority of two-thirds or more of the members present.
(3) The provision of the preceding paragraph does not preclude the House of Commoners from calling for the meeting of a joint committee of both Houses, provided for by law.
(4) Failure by the House of Officers to take final action within sixty (60) days after receipt of a bill passed by the House of Commoners, time in recess excepted, may be determined by the House of Commoners to constitute a rejection of the said bill by the House of Officers.

'''Article 60.''' The House of Officers shall have the power to review and amend the budget and other financial bills with specific regard to the Three Forges framework established in Article 86(2). When the House of Officers makes a decision different from that of the House of Commoners on the budget, the provisions of Article 59(2) shall apply.

'''Article 61.''' The House of Officers shall have the power to review treaties that concern economic policy, national security, or military cooperation, and may submit advisory opinions to the House of Commoners.

'''Article 62.''' Each House may conduct investigations in relation to government, and may demand the presence and testimony of witnesses, and the production of records.

'''Article 63.''' The Prime Minister and other Ministers of State may, at any time, appear in either House for the purpose of speaking on bills, regardless of whether they are members of the House or not. They must appear when their presence is required in order to give answers or explanations.

'''Article 64.''' The Diet shall set up an impeachment court from among the members of both Houses for the purpose of judging those judges against whom removal proceedings have been instituted. Matters relating to impeachment shall be provided by law.

==Chapter V: The Cabinet==

'''Article 65.''' Executive power shall be vested in the Cabinet.

'''Article 66.''' The Cabinet shall consist of the Prime Minister, who shall be its head, and other Ministers of State, as provided for by law.
(2) The Prime Minister and other Ministers of State must be civilians.
(3) The Cabinet, in the exercise of executive power, shall be collectively responsible to the Diet.

'''Article 67.''' The Prime Minister shall be designated from among the members of the Diet by a resolution of the Diet. This designation shall precede all other business.

'''Article 68.''' The Prime Minister shall appoint the Ministers of State. However, a majority of their number must be chosen from among the members of the Diet.

'''Article 69.''' If the House of Commoners passes a non-confidence resolution, or rejects a confidence resolution, the Cabinet shall resign en masse, unless the House of Commoners is dissolved within ten (10) days.

'''Article 70.''' When there is a vacancy in the post of Prime Minister, or upon the convocation of the Diet after a general election of members of the House of Commoners, the Cabinet shall resign en masse.

'''Article 71.''' In the cases mentioned in the two preceding articles, the Cabinet shall continue to perform its functions until the time when a new Prime Minister is appointed.

'''Article 72.''' The Prime Minister, representing the Cabinet, submits bills, reports on general national affairs and foreign relations to the Diet and exercises control and supervision over various administrative branches.

'''Article 73.''' The Cabinet, in addition to other general administrative functions, shall perform the following functions:
:Administer the law faithfully; conduct affairs of state.
:Manage foreign affairs.
:Conclude treaties. However, it shall obtain prior or, depending on circumstances, subsequent approval of the Diet.
:Administer the civil service, in accordance with standards established by law.
:Prepare the budget, and present it to the Diet.
:Enact executive directives in order to execute the provisions of this Constitution and of the law. However, it cannot include in such directives penal provisions except those authorized by law.
:Decide on general amnesty, special amnesty, commutation of punishment, reprieve, and restoration of rights.

'''Article 74.''' All laws and executive directives shall be signed by the competent Minister of State and countersigned by the Prime Minister.

'''Article 75.''' The Ministers of State, during their tenure of office, shall not be subject to legal action without the consent of the Prime Minister. However, the right to take that action is not impaired hereby.

==Chapter VI: The Judiciary==

'''Article 76.''' The whole judicial power is vested in a Supreme Court and in such inferior courts as are established by law.
(2) No extraordinary tribunal shall be established, nor shall any organ or agency of the Executive be given final judicial power.
(3) All judges shall be independent in the exercise of their conscience and shall be bound only by this Constitution and the laws.

'''Article 77.''' The Supreme Court is vested with the rule-making power under which it determines the rules of procedure and of practice, and of matters relating to attorneys, the internal discipline of the courts and the administration of judicial affairs.
(2) The Advocate General and the officers of that office shall be subject to the rule-making power of the Supreme Court.
(3) The Supreme Court may delegate the power to make rules for inferior courts to such courts.

'''Article 78.''' Judges shall not be removed except by public impeachment nor judicially disciplined except by judicial decision. No organ or agency of the Executive shall administer disciplinary action against judges.

'''Article 79.''' The Supreme Court shall consist of a Chief Justice and fourteen Associate Justices. The Chief Justice shall be designated by the Cabinet and appointed by the Emperor. Associate Justices shall be appointed by the Cabinet.
(2) The appointment of the justices of the Supreme Court shall be reviewed by the people at the first general election of members of the House of Commoners following their appointment, and shall be reviewed again at the first general election for that purpose held after the expiration of ten (10) years from their appointment, and in the same manner thereafter.
(3) In cases mentioned in the preceding paragraph, when the majority of voters favors the dismissal of a justice, he shall be dismissed.
(4) Matters pertaining to review shall be prescribed by law.
(5) Justices of the Supreme Court shall be retired upon the attainment of the age as fixed by law.

'''Article 80.''' Judges of inferior courts shall be appointed by the Cabinet from a list of persons nominated by the Supreme Court. The term of office of judges of inferior courts shall be ten (10) years, and they may be reappointed. However, they shall be retired upon the attainment of the age as fixed by law.

'''Article 81.''' The Supreme Court is the court of last resort with power to determine the constitutionality of any law, order, regulation or official act. The Court may assess not only the formal compliance of a law with this Constitution, but also whether the law, as applied, produces outcomes inconsistent with the principles of this Constitution.

'''Article 82.''' Trials shall be conducted and judgment declared publicly. Where a court determines that publicity is dangerous to public order or morals, a trial may be conducted privately, but trials of political offenses, offenses involving the press, or cases wherein the constitutional rights of the people are in question shall always be conducted publicly.

'''Article 82A.''' The Advocate General shall be established as an independent constitutional officer, separate from the judiciary and the executive, with the duty to represent the public interest before the courts. The Advocate General may bring actions before the Supreme Court for the determination of the constitutionality of any law, order, regulation or official act, and may intervene in any case where the constitutional rights of the people are in question.
(2) The Advocate General shall be appointed by the Cabinet with the consent of a majority of both Houses of the Diet, and shall serve for a term of ten (10) years, renewable once. The Advocate General shall not be removed except by public impeachment.
(3) The Advocate General shall have the power to investigate any matter within the jurisdiction of the courts, to compel the production of evidence, and to issue reports on matters of public interest. The Advocate General shall report annually to the Diet on the state of constitutional rights in Okaiken.

==Chapter VII: Finance==

'''Article 83.''' The power to administer national finances shall be exercised as the Diet shall determine.

'''Article 84.''' No new taxes shall be imposed or existing ones modified except by law or under such conditions as law may prescribe.

'''Article 85.''' No money shall be expended, nor shall the State obligate itself, except as authorized by the Diet.

'''Article 86.''' The Cabinet shall prepare and submit to the Diet for its consideration and decision a budget for each fiscal year.
(2) The budget shall address the three domains of national resilience: the First Forge (physical and digital infrastructure), the Second Forge (institutional and intellectual capacity), and the Third Forge (social welfare and national defense). No budget may allocate resources exclusively to one forge at the expense of the others, except in times of national emergency as determined by the Diet.

'''Article 87.''' In order to meet unforeseen deficiencies in the budget, a reserve fund may be authorized by the Diet to be expended upon the responsibility of the Cabinet. The Cabinet must give the Diet a report, for its approval, of all payments made from the reserve fund.

'''Article 88.''' All property of the Imperial Household shall belong to the State. All expenses of the Imperial Household shall be appropriated by the Diet in the budget.

'''Article 89.''' No public money or other property shall be expended or appropriated for the use, benefit or maintenance of any religious institution or association, or for any charitable, educational or benevolent enterprises not under the control of public authority.

'''Article 90.''' Final accounts of revenues and expenditures of the State shall be audited annually by the Audit Commission and submitted by the Cabinet to the Diet, together with the statement of audit, during the fiscal year immediately following the period covered. The organization and competency of the Audit Commission shall be determined by law.

'''Article 91.''' The Cabinet shall submit to the Diet, at regular intervals and at least semi-annually, reports on the state of national finances.

==Chapter VIII: The Electoral Commission==

'''Article 92.''' The Electoral Commission of Okaiken shall be established as an independent constitutional body, separate from the executive branch, the judiciary, and the Diet. The Commission shall administer all national elections, certify results, conduct mandatory audits, and investigate allegations of electoral fraud.

'''Article 93.''' Members of the Electoral Commission shall be appointed by the Diet through a supermajority vote of eighty percent (80%) of all members of each House. If the Diet cannot achieve an 80% supermajority for a given appointment within sixty (60) days of the vacancy, the Supreme Court may rule on the appointment and confirm the nominee by a simple majority of the Grand Bench.

'''Article 94.''' The Electoral Commission shall have the power to void election results in districts where fraud is proven and to order new elections. When the Commission certifies that electoral fraud has occurred on a scale sufficient to affect the outcome of a national election, the Emperor may dissolve the Diet and order new elections, in accordance with Article 1A(a).

'''Article 95.''' Electoral fraud—including vote-rigging, ballot stuffing, voter intimidation, and the falsification of election results—shall be a criminal offense punishable by imprisonment of not less than five years. Any public official convicted of electoral fraud shall be permanently disqualified from holding public office.

==Chapter IX: Emergency Referendum==

'''Article 96.''' An emergency referendum may be initiated when:
:(a) The Diet has failed to pass a budget within 120 days of the start of the fiscal year;
:(b) The Diet has failed to designate a Prime Minister within 30 days of a vacancy; or
:(c) A petition signed by at least ten percent (10%) of registered voters is submitted to the Electoral Commission.

'''Article 97.''' An emergency referendum may be called by the Emperor, by the Cabinet, or by the petition of citizens as described in Article 96(c).

'''Article 98.''' A constitutional amendment proposed by emergency referendum shall require the affirmative vote of sixty percent (60%) of all votes cast, with a minimum turnout of fifty percent (50%) of registered voters, to be ratified.

'''Article 99.''' Amendments ratified by emergency referendum shall be immediately promulgated by the Emperor and shall become part of this Constitution. The Diet shall be notified within 48 hours.

==Chapter X: Local Self-Government==

'''Article 100.''' Regulations concerning organization and operations of local public entities shall be fixed by law in accordance with the principle of local autonomy.

'''Article 101.''' The local public entities shall establish assemblies as their deliberative organs, in accordance with law.

'''Article 102.''' The chief executive officers of all local public entities, the members of their assemblies, and such other local officials as may be determined by law shall be elected by direct popular vote within their several communities.

'''Article 103.''' Within the limits of law, local public entities shall have the authority to manage their own property, affairs, and administration, and to enact their own regulations.

'''Article 104.''' A special law, applicable only to one local public entity, cannot be enacted by the Diet without the consent of the majority of the voters of the local public entity concerned, obtained in accordance with law.

==Chapter XI: Amendments==

'''Article 105.''' Amendment to this Constitution shall be initiated by the Diet, through a concurring vote of two-thirds or more of all the members of each House and shall thereupon be submitted to the people for ratification which shall require the affirmative vote of a majority of all votes cast thereon, at a special referendum or at such election as the Diet shall specify.
(2) Amendments when so ratified shall immediately be promulgated by the Emperor in the name of the people, as an integral part of this Constitution.
(3) Amendments may also be proposed and ratified through the emergency referendum process as defined in Chapter IX.

==Chapter XII: Supreme Law==

'''Article 106.''' The fundamental human rights by this Constitution guaranteed to the people of Okaiken are conferred upon this and future generations in trust, to be held for all time inviolate. The rights of the people are the conditions under which the nation endures; the duties of the people are the conditions under which the rights are sustained.

'''Article 107.''' This Constitution shall be the supreme law of the nation and no law, ordinance, imperial decree or other act of government, or part thereof, contrary to the provisions hereof, shall have legal force or validity.
(2) The treaties concluded by Okaiken and established laws of nations shall be faithfully observed.

'''Article 108.''' The Emperor or the Regent as well as Ministers of State, members of the Diet, judges, and all other public officials have the obligation to respect and uphold this Constitution.

==Chapter XIII: Supplementary Provisions==

'''Article 109.''' This Constitution shall be enforced as from the day when the period of six months will have elapsed counting from the day of its promulgation.
(2) The enactment of laws necessary for the enforcement of this Constitution, the election of members of the House of Officers, and the procedure for the convocation of the Diet and other preparatory procedures for the enforcement of this Constitution may be executed before the day prescribed in the preceding paragraph.

'''Article 110.''' The Saito Constitution of 1870 is hereby repealed.

'''Article 111.''' All laws, ordinances, imperial decrees and other acts of government conflicting with this Constitution shall be of no effect from the date of enforcement of this Constitution.

'''Article 112.''' The terms of office of all officials elected or appointed before the enforcement of this Constitution shall continue until the expiration of their respective terms, unless otherwise provided by law.

'''Article 113.''' The organization, authority, and procedures of the Electoral Commission shall be established by law within one year of the enforcement of this Constitution.

'''Article 114.''' The system of national service established by Article 26C shall be implemented by law within two years of the enforcement of the Eighth Amendment (1982).

